# Layering

## Layering {.smaller}

::: {.columns .pv4}
::: {.column width="60%"}
-   ggplot2 uses a [layered]{.b .blue} grammar of graphics
    -   We can keep [stacking geoms]{.b .green} on top

::: {.fragment .mt1}
-   Layering adds a lot of possibilities
    -   We can convey [more complex]{.b .green} ideas
    -   We can learn more about our data
:::

::: {.fragment .mt1}
-   But we can still describe these graphics
    -   Just [describe each layer]{.b .green} in turn
    -   And describe the layers' ordering
:::
:::

::: {.column .tc .pv5 width="40%"}
{{< lif "../../icons/layers.json" trigger=hover colors=secondary:#2a76dd class=rc >}}
:::
:::

## The Smooth Geom


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Left: Point geom plots all the observations
p1 <- ggplot(mpg, aes(x = displ, y = hwy)) + geom_point()

# Right: Smooth geom plots a local line-of-best-fit
p2 <- ggplot(mpg, aes(x = displ, y = hwy)) + geom_smooth()
```
:::



::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](b_Slides_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-3-1.png){width=1536}
:::
:::


## Overlapping the Layers


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Adding additional geoms stacks their layers on top
ggplot(mpg, aes(x = displ, y = hwy)) +
  geom_point() + 
  geom_smooth()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](b_Slides_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){width=960}
:::
:::


